    "content": "Historically Black Colleges and Universities (HBCUs) are accredited institutions established before 1964 to provide higher education to African Americans. Over 100 HBCUs operate in the U.S., primarily in the South, producing 20% of Black graduates. Top-ranked institutions include Howard University, Spelman College, and Morehouse College. Key Details About HBCUs: Top-Ranked HBCUs: Howard University (DC) is frequently ranked #1, known for top programs and Spelman College (GA) is a leading liberal arts college. Other top schools include Morehouse College (GA), {Link: Hampton University https://www.usnews.com/best-colleges/rankings/hbcu} (VA), and Florida A&M University. Total Number: There are over 100 HBCUs in the United States, including public and private four-year institutions and two-year colleges. History: Founded in the 19th and early 20th centuries, they were established to provide education to people of African descent, particularly in the South, when they were otherwise denied access to higher education, notes the Searchable Museum. Notable Alumni: Famous alumni include Martin Luther King Jr. (Morehouse College), Toni Morrison (Howard University), Spike Lee (Morehouse College), and Vice President Kamala Harris (Howard University), as shown in this YouTube video. Admissions & Cost: While some HBCUs are selective (e.g., Spelman with a ~23% acceptance rate), many are accessible, and they often offer more affordable tuition compared to other institutions. Top 10 Ranked HBCUs (US News & World Report): Howard University Spelman College Howard University (Various Rankings) {Link: Hampton University https://www.usnews.com/best-colleges/rankings/hbcu} Morehouse College {Link: Xavier University of Louisiana https://www.usnews",
